      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To evaluate the effect of heterotopic painful stimulation (HPS) on the cutaneous silent period (CSP) and the withdrawal flexor reflex (WFR) in the upper limbs, in an attempt to better identify the nociceptive component of the CSP and its functional relationship with the flexor reflex."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "The CSP at different stimulus intensities, the WFR and the H/M ratio were studied in 12 healthy adults. Neurophysiological measurements were recorded in the following 4 conditions: (1) control session; (2) non-painful session (dipping hand in water at 25 degrees C); (3) painful (cold pressor test, CPT); and (4) after-effect (3-8 min after taking hand out of water at 5-6 degrees C)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "During the CPT, the duration of the high-threshold CSP was approximately 23% shorter than the baseline value, the high-threshold CSP latency was approximately 10% longer than the baseline value, and the mean RIII reflex area was approximately 40% smaller than the baseline reflex area (all P<0.05). A significant correlation was found between the percentage decrease in the CSP duration and the WFR area (r=0.61, P<0.05)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Our findings indicate that the HPS specifically inhibits both the high-threshold CSP and the WFR, thereby providing further evidence that these cutaneous reflexes are functionally and anatomically related, and that they represent different aspects of a complex nocifensive response."
        }
      ]},
